 \section{Related work}
 Deep ensembles train and average independent predictors \citep{lakshminarayanan2017simple}. TreeNets share early computation before branching \citep{lee2015heads}. GNNM instead creates distinct member states before graph propagation and shares the weights applied to those states. BatchEnsemble provides the multiplicative factorization \citep{wen2020batchensemble}, and TabM studies related models for tabular data \citep{gorishniy2025tabm}. Kim's master's thesis applies BatchEnsemble inside GNN layers \citep{kim2023efficient}. Its bibliographic record is supplied, and our added-factor comparison is not an exact reproduction of that implementation.
 
-Other approaches target different costs. GEENI approximates constituent GNNs to reduce ensemble latency \citep{nagarajan2022geeni}, and ensembles can be distilled into one predictor \citep{zhang2024e2gnn}. Graph mixtures learn how to choose or weight experts \citep{wang2023graph,deng2025mixture}, whereas GNNM uniformly averages member scores. MIMO trains multiple predictions within one forward pass \citep{havasi2021training}, while Packed-Ensembles use grouped operations to run structured ensembles together \citep{laurent2023packed}. The native SAGE comparison includes one parameter-budget Packed-Ensembles adaptation. MIMO and the other untested efficient methods remain relevant alternatives; the experiments do not establish a general efficiency ranking. The supplement gives the tested alternatives, including a local MC Dropout comparison, and documents their scope.
